--TEST--
a tainted path and a tainted argument list are refused at both members that start a program, so a name the program did not choose cannot become the command — `rule:core-classes/process-is-argv-only`, `rule:core-classes/process-spawn`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/process-is-argv-only`: the path and every element of the argument array are
// plain `string`, so a tainted value needs a checked conversion or an explicit
// launderer before either member takes it. What this file asks is that `run`
// and `spawn` *agree* — the second member came later, and one that grew a
// qualifier of its own would still read right on its own line and fail here.
tainted string $t = "/bin/echo";
array<tainted string> $words = [$t];
array<string> $none = [];

// The path. An argument list the program wrote does not rescue it: what is
// refused is the name of the program, which is the whole of what a command
// injection is.
var $ran = Core\Process::run($t, $none);
var $started = Core\Process::spawn($t, $none);

// One element of the list, under a path the program wrote. The array carries
// no nesting mark of its own — `array<tainted string>` is simply not
// `array<string>`, which is the rule's own sentence — so this is refused for
// the element's qualifier and not for anything the element says.
var $second = Core\Process::run("/bin/echo", $words);
var $third = Core\Process::spawn("/bin/echo", $words);

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:30
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:15:36
%A
error[E0401]: expected `array<string>`, found `array<tainted string>`
   --> case.nvs:21:46
%A
error[E0401]: expected `array<string>`, found `array<tainted string>`
   --> case.nvs:22:47
%A
